== Symmetry and Proportion

X. Another thing in which truly gracious and holy affections differ from false ones is #emph[beautiful symmetry and proportion.]

Not that the symmetry of the virtues and gracious affections of the saints is perfect in this life. It is often defective in many things: through the imperfection of grace, for lack of proper instruction, through errors in judgment, or some particular unhappiness of natural temper, or defects in upbringing, and many other disadvantages that might be mentioned. But there is in no way that monstrous disproportion in the gracious affections and the various parts of true religion in the saints which is very commonly to be seen in the false religion and counterfeit graces of hypocrites.

In the truly holy affections of the saints is found that proportion which is the natural consequence of the universality of their sanctification. They have the whole image of Christ on them. They have “taken off the old man” and “have put on the new man,” entire in all his parts and members. “God was pleased to have all His fullness dwell in Him”: there is every grace in him. He is “full of grace and truth,” and those who are Christ’s “received of his fullness, and grace for grace” (John 1:14, 16). That is, every grace that is in Christ is in them: “grace for grace,” that is, grace answering to grace. There is no grace in Christ without its image in believers to answer it. The image is a true image, and there is something of the same beautiful proportion in the image as in the original: there is feature for feature and member for member. There is symmetry and beauty in God’s workmanship. The natural body that God has made consists of many members, and all are in beautiful proportion. So it is in the new man, which consists of various graces and affections. The body of one who was born a perfect child may fall short of exact proportion through illness, and through the weakness and wounds of some of its members. Yet the disproportion is in no way like that of those who are born deformed.

It is with hypocrites as it was with #emph[Ephraim] long ago, at a time when God greatly complains of their hypocrisy: “Ephraim is an unturned cake” (Hos 7), half baked and half raw. There is commonly no uniformity at all in their affections.

In many of them there is a great partiality with regard to the several kinds of religious affections: great affections in some things, and no proportion at all in others. A holy hope and a holy fear go together in the saints, as has been observed from Ps 33:18 and 147:11. But in some of these persons there is the most confident hope, while they are empty of reverence, self-suspicion and caution, and have to a great degree cast off fear. In the saints, joy and holy fear go together, however great the joy may be, as it was with the disciples on that joyful morning of Christ’s resurrection. Matt 28:8: “So they hurried away from the tomb in fear and great joy.” #footnote[Renewed care and diligence follow the sealings of the Spirit. Now the soul is at the feet of Christ, as #emph[Mary] was at the tomb, #emph[with fear and great joy.] He who travels the road with a rich treasure about him is afraid of a thief in every bush. #emph[Flavel]’s sacramental meditations, Medi. 4.] But many of these rejoice without trembling: their joy is of a sort that is truly opposed to godly fear.

But in particular, one great difference between saints and hypocrites is this: the joy and comfort of the former is attended with godly sorrow and mourning for sin. They have sorrow not only to prepare them for their first comfort, but also after they are comforted and their joy is established. So it is foretold of the church of God that they would mourn and loathe themselves for their sins after they had returned from the captivity and were settled in the land of #emph[Canaan,] the land of rest, the land that flows with milk and honey. Ezek 20:42, 43: “Then you#super[+] will know that I am the #smallcaps[Lord], when I bring you#super[+] into the land of #emph[Israel,] the land that I swore to give your#super[+] fathers. There you#super[+] will remember your#super[+] ways and all the deeds with which you#super[+] have defiled yourselves, and you#super[+] will loathe yourselves for all the evils you#super[+] have done.” So also in Ezekiel 16:61, 62, 63. A true saint is like a little child in this respect. He never had any godly sorrow before he was born again, but since then he often has it in exercise. So a little child, before it is born, while it remains in darkness, never cries; but as soon as it sees the light it begins to cry, and from then on it is often crying. Christ has borne our griefs and carried our sorrows, so that we are freed from the #emph[sorrow of punishment,] and may now sweetly feed on the comforts Christ has purchased for us. Yet that does not prevent our feeding on these comforts from being attended with the #emph[sorrow of repentance.] So in the past the children of #emph[Israel] were commanded always to eat the Passover lamb with bitter herbs. #footnote[If repentance accompanies faith, it is no presumption to believe. Many know their sin, and so they believe in Christ and trust in Christ, and there is an end of their faith. But what confession and sorrow for sin, what more love to Christ follows this faith? Truly none. Indeed, their faith is the reason they have none. For they think: If I trust in Christ to forgive me, he will do it, and there is an end of the matter. Truly this hedge-faith, this bramble-faith, which catches hold of Christ and pricks and scratches Christ by more impenitence and more contempt of him, is mere presumption, which will one day be burned up and destroyed by the fire of God’s jealousy. Shame on that faith which serves only to keep a man from being tormented before his time! Your sins would be your sorrows, if your faith did not quiet you. But suppose faith is accompanied with repentance, mourning for sin, and a higher esteem of God’s grace in Christ, so that nothing breaks your heart more than the thought of Christ’s unchangeable love to one so vile; and this love makes you love much, and love him the more; and as your sin increases, so you desire your love to increase; and now the stream of your thoughts runs on how you may live to him who died for you. This was #emph[Mary]’s faith, who sat at Christ’s feet weeping, washing them with her tears, and loving much, because much was forgiven. #emph[Shepard]’s sound believer. P. 128, 129. You will know godly sorrow (says Dr. #emph[Preston,] in his discourse on #emph[Paul]’s conversion) by its continuance. It is constant; but worldly sorrow is only a passion of the mind: it changes, it does not last. Though for the present it may be violent and strong and work much outwardly, it comes only in fits and does not continue, like a flash flood, which violently overflows the banks for the present, but will go away again; it is not always so. But godly sorrow is like a spring, which keeps running still, winter and summer, wet and dry, in heat and cold, early and late. So this godly sorrow is still the same in a regenerate man: take him when you will, he is still sorrowing for sin. This godly sorrow stands like the center of the earth, which does not move, but still remains. I am persuaded that many a man’s heart is kept from breaking and mourning because of this. He says (perhaps) that he is a vile sinner, “but I trust in Christ,” and so on. If they do go to Christ to destroy their sin, this makes them more secure in their sin. For (they say) “I cannot help it, and Christ must do all.” Whereas faith makes the soul mourn after the Lord all the more. #emph[Shep.] Parable of the ten virgins, Part II. P. 168.] True saints are spoken of in Scripture not only as those who have mourned for sin, but as those who do mourn, whose way it still is to mourn. Matt 5:4: “Blessed are those who mourn, for they will be comforted.”

In hypocrites there is often not only an essential deficiency in the various kinds of religious affections, but also a strange partiality and disproportion in the same affections with regard to different objects.

So, as to the affection of #emph[love,] some make high claims and a great show of love to God and Christ, and perhaps have been greatly affected by what they have heard or thought about them. But they do not have a spirit of love and benevolence toward men. They are inclined to contention, envy, revenge and evil-speaking. Perhaps they will let an old grudge against a neighbor rest in their hearts for seven years together, if not twice seven years, living in real ill will and bitterness of spirit toward him. And perhaps in their dealings with their neighbors they are not very strict and conscientious in keeping the rule of #emph[doing to others as they would have others do to them.] 1 John 4:20: “If anyone says, ‘I love God,’ but hates his brother, he is a liar. For anyone who does not love his brother, whom he has seen, cannot love God, whom he has not seen.” And on the other hand, there are others who appear to have a great deal of benevolence toward men, and are very good-natured and generous in their way, but have no love to God.

And as to love to men, some have overflowing affections toward some people, but their love is far from being of so extensive and universal a nature as a truly Christian love is. They are full of tender affection toward some, and full of bitterness toward others. They are bound to their own party, to those who approve of them, love them and admire them, but are fierce against those who oppose and dislike them. Matt 5:45, 46: “that you#super[+] may be sons of your#super[+] Father in heaven. He causes His sun to rise on the evil and the good. … If you#super[+] love those who love you#super[+], what reward will you#super[+] get? Do not even tax collectors do the same?” Some show great affection to their neighbors, and claim to be delighted beyond words with the company of the children of God #emph[away from home,] and at the same time are disagreeable and churlish toward their wives and other close relatives #emph[at home,] and very negligent of the duties of those relations. And then there is the great love to sinners and opposers of religion, and the great concern for their souls, that appears in some, even to extreme distress and agony. It singles out one particular person from among a multitude as its object, while there is at the same time no general compassion for sinners who are in equally miserable circumstances, or none but what is monstrously out of proportion. This seems not to be of the nature of a gracious affection. Not that I suppose it at all strange that pity for the perishing souls of sinners should rise to a degree of agony, if other things answer to it; or that a truly gracious compassion for souls should be exercised much more toward some persons than toward others who are equally miserable, especially on some particular occasions. Many things may happen at such a time to fix the mind and affect the heart with respect to a particular person. And no doubt some saints have been in great distress for the souls of particular persons, so as to be, as it were, in labor for them. But suppose persons appear at particular times in racking agonies for the soul of some single person, far beyond what has usually been heard or read of in eminent saints, and yet appear to have a spirit of meek and fervent love, charity and compassion toward mankind in general in a far less degree than those saints had. Such agonies, I say, are greatly to be suspected, for reasons already given: namely, that the Spirit of God usually gives graces and gracious affections in a beautiful symmetry and proportion.

And as there is a monstrous disproportion in the love of some in its exercises toward different persons, so there is in their seeming exercises of love toward the same persons. Some men show a love to others as to their outward man: they are generous with their worldly goods and often give to the poor, but have no love for or concern for the souls of men. Others claim a great love for men’s souls, but are not compassionate and charitable toward their bodies. Making a great show of love, pity and distress for souls costs them nothing; but to show mercy to men’s bodies they must part with money out of their pockets. But a true Christian love to our brothers extends to both their souls and their bodies. In this it is like the love and compassion of Jesus Christ. He showed mercy to men’s souls by laboring for them in preaching the gospel to them, and showed mercy to their bodies by going about doing good, healing every kind of sickness and disease among the people. We have a remarkable instance in Mark 6:34 and following of Christ having compassion on both men’s souls and their bodies at once, and showing compassion by feeding both: “When Jesus stepped ashore and saw a large crowd, He had compassion on them, because they were like sheep without a shepherd. And He began to teach them many things.” Here was his compassion for their souls. And in what follows we have an account of his compassion for their bodies, because they had been a long while with #emph[nothing to eat:] he fed five thousand of them with five loaves and two fish. And if the compassion of professing Christians toward others does not work in the same ways, it is a sign that it is no true Christian compassion.

Furthermore, it is a sign that affections are not of the right sort if persons seem to be much affected by the bad qualities of their fellow Christians, such as the coldness and lifelessness of other saints, but are not affected in any proportion by their own defects and corruptions. A true Christian may be affected by the coldness and lack of savor in other saints, and may mourn much over it. But at the same time he is not so apt to be affected by the badness of anybody’s heart as by that of his own. This is most in his view; this he is quickest to discern; this he sees most of the aggravations of, and is most ready to cry out against. And it takes a lesser degree of virtue to bring a man to pity himself and be concerned at his own calamities than to be rightly affected by the calamities of others. And if men have not attained the lesser, we may determine that they never attained the greater.

And here, by the way, I would observe that it may be laid down as a general rule that if persons claim to have come to high attainments in religion but have never yet reached the lesser attainments, it is a sign of a vain claim. For example, persons may claim that they have got beyond mere #emph[morality] to live a #emph[spiritual] and #emph[divine] life, but really have not come so far as to be #emph[moral] persons. Or they may claim to be greatly affected by the wickedness of their hearts, and yet not be affected by the plain violations of God’s commands in their practice, which is a lesser attainment. Or they may claim to have been brought even to be willing to be damned for the glory of God, and yet have no readiness to suffer a little in their property, their reputation and their worldly convenience for the sake of their duty. Or they may claim that they are not afraid to venture their souls on Christ and commit their all to God, trusting his bare word and the faithfulness of his promises for their eternal welfare, and yet at the same time not have enough confidence in God to dare to trust him with a little of their property given to godly and charitable uses. When it is so with persons, I say, their claims are plainly vain. A man on a journey who imagines he has got far beyond a certain place on his road, and never yet came to it, must be mistaken. And the man who never yet got halfway up the hill has not yet reached the top. But this is by the way.

What has been observed of the affection of #emph[love] is also to be observed of #emph[other] religious affections. Those that are true extend in some proportion to the various things that are their due and proper objects; but when they are false, they are commonly strangely out of proportion. So it is with religious #emph[desires] and longings. In the saints these go out to things that are spiritual and excellent in general, and in some proportion to their excellence, importance or necessity, or to the saints’ own close concern in them. But in false longings it is often far otherwise. They will strangely run with impatient vehemence after something of less importance, while other things of greater importance are neglected. For instance, some persons are from time to time seized with a vehement inclination, and an unaccountably violent pressure, to tell others what they experience and to exhort others. Yet at the same time there is no inclination in any measure equal to it toward other things that true Christianity tends to as much as, indeed more than, that: such as pouring out the soul before God in secret, earnest prayer and praise to him, and more conformity to him, and living more to his glory, and so on. We read in Scripture of #emph[groans too deep for words,] and of #emph[the soul consumed with longing,] and of #emph[longings, thirstings] and #emph[pantings,] much more often for these latter things than for the former.

So too with #emph[hatred] and #emph[zeal.] When these come from right principles, they are against sin in general, in some proportion to the degree of its sinfulness. Ps 119:104: “I hate every false way.” So verse 128. But a false hatred and zeal against sin is against some particular sin only. So some seem to be very zealous against profaneness and pride in clothing, who themselves are notorious for covetousness and stinginess, and perhaps for backbiting, envy toward those above them, a turbulent spirit toward rulers, and rooted ill will toward those who have injured them. False zeal is against the sins of others, while men have no zeal against their own sins. But he who has true zeal exercises it chiefly against his own sins, though he also shows a proper zeal against prevailing and dangerous iniquity in others. And some claim to have a great abhorrence of their own sins of heart, and cry out much about their inward corruption; and yet they make light of sins in practice, and seem to commit them without much restraint or remorse, though these involve sin in both heart and life.

As there is a much greater disproportion in the exercises of false affections than in those of true ones with regard to different objects, so there is also with regard to different times. True Christians are not always the same. Indeed, there is a very great difference at different times, and the best have reason to be greatly ashamed of their unsteadiness. Yet there is in no way, in the hearts of those who are true #emph[virgins, who follow the Lamb wherever He goes,] the instability and inconstancy there is in false-hearted professing Christians. The righteous man is truly said to be one #emph[whose heart is steadfast, trusting in God] (Ps 112:7), to have #emph[his heart established by grace] (Heb 13:9), and to #emph[hold to his way.] Job 17:9: “Yet a righteous one holds to his way, and the one with clean hands grows stronger.” It is spoken of as a mark of the hypocrisy of the Jewish church that they #emph[were like a swift young camel, running here and there in her ways.]

If, then, persons are religious only by fits and starts, it is a sign that their affections are unsound. Suppose they now and then seem to be raised up to the clouds in their affections, and then suddenly fall down again, lose everything, and become quite careless and carnal, and this is their way of carrying on religion. Suppose they appear greatly moved and mightily engaged in religion only in extraordinary seasons: in the time of a remarkable outpouring of the Spirit, or some other uncommon dispensation of providence, or on the real or supposed receipt of some great mercy, when they have received some extraordinary earthly mercy, or suppose that they are newly converted, or have lately had what they call a great discovery. But they quickly return to such a frame that their hearts are chiefly on other things, and the prevailing bent of their hearts and the stream of their affections is ordinarily toward the things of this world. They are like the children of #emph[Israel] in the wilderness. Their affections were highly raised by what God had done for them at the Red Sea, and they sang his praise, and soon began craving the meat pots of #emph[Egypt.] But then again, when they came to Mount #emph[Sinai] and saw the great displays God made of himself there, they seemed to be greatly engaged again, and very eager to enter into covenant with God, saying, “All that the #smallcaps[Lord] has spoken we will do, and we will be obedient”; but then they quickly made themselves a golden calf. When it is so with persons, I say, it is a sign of the unsoundness of their affections. #footnote[Dr. #emph[Owen] (on the Spirit, book III. Chap. Ii. § 18.), speaking of a common work of the Spirit, says: This work operates greatly on the affections. We have given instances in fear, sorrow, joy and delight about spiritual things, which are stirred up and set to work by it. But it still falls short of a thorough work on the affections themselves in two things. For 1st, it does not #emph[fix] them. And 2nd, it does not #emph[fill] them. 1. It is required that our affections be fixed on heavenly and spiritual things, and true grace will bring it about. Col 3:1, 2: “Therefore, since you#super[+] have been raised #emph[with] Christ, strive for the things above, where Christ is seated at the right hand of God. Set your#super[+] affections on things above.” The joys, the fears, the hopes, the sorrows concerning spiritual and eternal things which the work mentioned before produces are fleeting, uncertain and unstable, not only as to their degrees but as to their very being. Sometimes they are like a river ready to overflow its banks: men cannot help pouring them out on every occasion. And sometimes they are like waters that fail: not a drop comes from them. Sometimes they are hot and sometimes cold, sometimes up and sometimes down, sometimes all heaven and sometimes all world, without evenness, without stability. But true grace fixes the affections on spiritual things. As to the degrees of their exercise, there may be, and there is, a great variety in them, according as they are stirred up, aided and assisted by grace and its means, or obstructed and hindered by temptations and distractions coming between. But the constant bent and inclination of renewed affections is toward spiritual things, as the Scripture everywhere testifies, and as experience confirms. There is (says Dr. #emph[Preston]) a certain love by fits which God does not accept: when men come and offer God great promises, like the waves of the sea, as big as mountains. Oh, they think they will do much for God! But their minds change, and they become like those high waves which at last fall level with the other waters. If a man should offer you great kindnesses, and you should afterward come to him to make use of him, and he should look at you strangely, as if he had never been acquainted with you, how would you value such love? If we are now on, now off, in our love, God will not value such love. Discourse on the divine love of Christ. Mr. #emph[Flavel,] speaking of these changeable professing Christians, says: These professors have more of the moon than of the sun: little light, less heat, and many changes. They deceive many, indeed they deceive themselves, but they cannot deceive God. They lack the ballast and settledness in themselves that would have kept them tight and steady. Touchstone of sincerity, ch. 2. § 2.] They are like the waters during a shower of rain, which during the shower and a little after run like a brook and flow abundantly, but are soon quite dry; and when another shower comes, they will flow again. But a true saint is like a stream from a living spring, which, though it may be greatly increased by a shower of rain and diminished in time of drought, still runs constantly (John 4:14: “the water I give him will become in him a fount of water springing up,” and so on). Or he is like a tree planted by such a stream, which has a constant supply at the root and is always green, even in time of the greatest drought. Jer 17:7, 8: “But blessed is the man who trusts in the #smallcaps[Lord], whose confidence is in Him. He is like a tree planted by the waters that sends out its roots toward the stream. It does not fear when the heat comes, and its leaves are always green. It does not worry in a year of drought, nor does it cease to produce fruit.” Many hypocrites are like comets, which appear for a while with a mighty blaze, but are very unsteady and irregular in their motion (and are therefore called wandering stars, #emph[Jude] 13); their blaze soon disappears, and they appear only once in a great while. But the true saints are like the fixed stars, which, though they rise and set and are often clouded, are steadfast in their orbit, and may truly be said to shine with a constant light. Hypocritical affections are like a forced motion, like that of the air when it is moved by winds (#emph[Jude] 12). But gracious affections are more a natural motion, like the stream of a river, which has many turns this way and that, and may meet with obstacles, and run more freely and swiftly in some places than in others, yet in general tends the same way with a steady and constant course until it reaches the ocean.

And as there is a strange unevenness and disproportion in false affections at different #emph[times,] so there often is in different #emph[places.] Some are greatly affected from time to time when they are in company, but have nothing that bears any proportion to it in secret: in close meditation, secret prayer, and conversing with God when they are alone and separated from all the world. #footnote[The Lord is neglected in secret, yet honored openly; because there is no wind in their rooms to blow their sails, and so there they stand still. That is why many men keep their profession when they lose their affection. By the one they have a name to live (and that is enough), though their hearts are dead. And that is why, as long as you love and commend them, they love you; but if not, they will forsake you. They were warm only by another’s fire, and so, having no principle of life within, they soon grow dead. This is the water that turns a Pharisee’s mill. #emph[Shepard]’s Par. Part I. P. 180. The hypocrite (says Mr. #emph[Flavel]) is not for the private room, but the synagogue (Matt 6:5, 6). It is not his food and drink to withdraw from the noise of the world to enjoy God in secret. Touchstone of sincerity, ch. 7. § 2. Dr. #emph[Ames,] in his cases of conscience, Lib. III. Chapter 5., speaks of it as a thing by which sincerity may be known, that persons are obedient in the absence as well as in the presence of onlookers, in secret as much as, indeed more than, in public, citing Phil 2:12 and Matt 6:6.] A true Christian doubtless delights in religious fellowship and Christian conversation, and finds much in it to affect his heart. But he also delights at times to withdraw from all mankind, to converse with God in solitary places. And this too has its own advantages for fixing his heart and engaging its affections. True religion disposes persons to be much alone in solitary places for holy meditation and prayer. So it worked in #emph[Isaac] (Gen 24:63). And, which is much more, so it worked in Jesus Christ. How often do we read of his withdrawing into mountains and solitary places for holy communion with his Father? It is difficult to conceal great affections, but gracious affections are of a much more silent and secret nature than counterfeit ones. So it is with the gracious sorrow of the saints. So it is with their sorrow for their own sins. #footnote[Mr. #emph[Flavel,] in listing the things in which the sorrow of saints over their sins is distinguished from the sorrow of hypocrites, says: Their troubles for sin are more private and silent troubles than others’ are; their sore runs in the night. Touchstone of sincerity, ch. 6. § v.] So the future gracious mourning of true penitents, at the beginning of the glory of the latter days, is represented as being so secret as to be hidden from the companions of their bosom. Zech 12:12, 13, 14: “The land will mourn, each clan on its own: the clan of the house of #emph[David] on its own, and their wives on their own; the clan of the house of #emph[Nathan] on its own, and their wives on their own; the clan of the house of #emph[Levi] on its own, and their wives on their own; the clan of #emph[Shimei] on its own, and their wives on their own; and all the remaining clans, each clan on its own, and their wives on their own.” So it is with their sorrow for the sins of others. The saints’ pains and labor for the souls of sinners are chiefly in secret places. Jer 13:17: “But if you#super[+] do not listen, I will weep in secret because of your#super[+] pride. My eyes will overflow with tears, because the #smallcaps[Lord]’s flock has been taken captive.” So it is with gracious joys: in this respect, as in others, they are #emph[hidden manna] (Rev 2:17). The psalmist seems to speak of his sweetest comforts as those that were to be had in secret. Ps 63:5: “My soul is satisfied as with the richest of foods; with joyful lips my mouth will praise You. When I remember You on my bed, I think of You through the watches of the night.” Christ calls his bride away from the world into secluded places, so that he may give her his sweetest love. Song 7:11, 12: “Come, my beloved, let us go to the countryside; let us spend the night among the wildflowers. … There I will give you my love.” The most eminent divine favors the saints received that we read of in Scripture came in their times alone. The chief manifestations God made of himself and his covenant mercy to #emph[Abraham] were when he was alone, apart from his large household, as anyone who reads his history carefully will judge. #emph[Isaac] received that special gift of God to him, #emph[Rebekah,] who was so great a comfort to him, and by whom he obtained the promised seed, while he was walking alone, meditating in the field. #emph[Jacob] had withdrawn for secret prayer when Christ came to him, and he wrestled with him and obtained the blessing. God revealed himself to #emph[Moses] in the bush when he was in a solitary place in the desert, at Mount #emph[Horeb] (Exod 3, at the beginning). And afterward, when God showed him his glory, and he was admitted to the highest degree of communion with God he ever enjoyed, he was alone on the same mountain, and stayed there forty days and forty nights, and then came down with his face shining. God came to those great prophets #emph[Elijah] and #emph[Elisha,] and conversed freely with them, chiefly when they were alone. #emph[Elijah] conversed alone with God at Mount #emph[Sinai,] as #emph[Moses] did. And when Jesus Christ had his greatest foretaste of his future glory, when he was transfigured, it was not when he was with the multitude, or with the twelve disciples. He had withdrawn into a solitary place on a mountain with only three chosen disciples, and he charged them to tell no one until he had risen from the dead. When the angel #emph[Gabriel] came to the blessed virgin, and when the Holy Spirit came upon her and the power of the Most High overshadowed her, she seems to have been alone, and to have been hidden from the world in this matter. Her nearest and dearest earthly friend, #emph[Joseph,] who was engaged to her (though he was a just man), knew nothing of it. And she who first shared in the joy of Christ’s resurrection was alone with Christ at the tomb (#emph[John] 20). And when the beloved disciple was favored with those wonderful visions of Christ, and of his future dealings with the church and the world, he was alone on the island of #emph[Patmos.] Not that we do not also have instances of great privileges the saints have received when they were with others, or that there is not much in Christian conversation, and in social and public worship, that tends greatly to refresh and gladden the hearts of the saints. But all I aim at in what has been said is to show that it is the nature of true grace that, however much it loves Christian company in its place, it delights in a special way in being alone and in secret communion with God. So if persons appear greatly engaged in social religion but little in the religion of the private room, and are often highly affected when they are with others but little moved when they have none but God and Christ to converse with, it looks very dark for their religion.
